\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {replaced}{Replaced~(rev): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{For each fixed box size and particle number, we keep the background cosmology, numerical settings, and MonofonIC random seed identical across the matched PL and BT runs. The main PL, BT\_soft, and BT\_deep \(25\,h^{-1}{\rm  Mpc}\), \(1024^3\) simulations therefore share the same initial phases. This lets us test the effect of enhancing the small-scale primordial power-spectrum template after it is passed through MonofonIC, rather than comparing different random realizations.}}{4}{section*.40}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {replaced}{Replaced~(rev): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{As a basic consistency check on the initial conditions, we compare the BT enhancement scales with the particle-grid scale. For the main \(25\,h^{-1}{\rm  Mpc}\), \(1024^3\) runs, the inter-particle spacing is \(L_{\rm  box}/1024=0.0244\,h^{-1}{\rm  Mpc}\), giving \(k_{\rm  Ny}\simeq 128.7\,h\,{\rm  Mpc}^{-1}\). The two pivots, \(k_p=1\) and \(10\,h\,{\rm  Mpc}^{-1}\), therefore lie factors of \(\simeq 129\) and \(\simeq 12.9\) below the particle-grid Nyquist scale. Equivalently, their pivot wavelengths are \(\lambda _p=2\pi /k_p\simeq 6.3\) and \(0.63\,h^{-1}{\rm  Mpc}\), corresponding to about \(257\) and \(26\) inter-particle spacings. Thus the imposed breaks in the input spectrum lie well inside the sampled Fourier range of the fiducial initial conditions. We also evaluate the dimensionless input power, \(\Delta ^2(k,z_i)=k^3P_{\rm  lin}(k,z_i)/(2\pi ^2)\); over \(k\le k_{\rm  Ny}\), the largest value among the BT input spectra is \(\Delta ^2\simeq 5.8\times 10^{-5}\) for BT\_soft, so the enhanced modes are still deeply in the linear regime at \(z_i=200\). This check verifies the placement of the imposed input-spectrum break relative to the particle grid; it is not a full nonlinear convergence test.}}{4}{section*.41}\protected@file@percent }
