\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {replaced}{Replaced~(rev): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{combined summary in Table~\ref {tab:simulation_parameters} lists both the PL/BT model parameters and the numerical setup. The main PL and BT simulations use the same box size, particle number, mass resolution, force softening, and matched initial phases. The comparison therefore isolates the response to the BT enhancement in the primordial power spectrum as generated through MonofonIC, not a change in the dynamical evolution algorithm.}}{4}{section*.50}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \replaced [id=rev]{Input linear matter power spectra at \(z_i=200\)}{Input primordial matter power spectra} used to generate the PL, BT\_soft, and BT\_deep initial conditions. \replaced [id=rev]{The PL spectrum provides the standard power-law baseline. The BT spectra use the same target \(\sigma _8\) normalization and keep the PL/Eisenstein shape below their pivot scales, up to the small global normalization applied by MonofonIC; above the pivot, they depart from PL.}{The PL spectrum provides the standard power-law baseline, while the BT spectra keep the large-scale normalization fixed and depart from PL only above their respective pivot scales.} BT\_soft departs from PL over a broader range of mildly nonlinear modes, whereas BT\_deep confines the modification to smaller scales. The spectra are evaluated at the initial redshift, \(z_i=200\). \replaced [id=rev]{For the main \(25\,h^{-1}{\rm  Mpc}\), \(1024^3\) runs, \(k_{\rm  Ny}\simeq 129\,h\,{\rm  Mpc}^{-1}\), so the \(k_p=1\) and \(10\,h\,{\rm  Mpc}^{-1}\) pivots are well below the particle-grid Nyquist scale.}{For the main $25\,h^{-1}\mathrm  {Mpc}$, $1024^3$ runs, the particle-grid Nyquist scale is \(k_{\rm  Ny}\simeq 129\,h\,\mathrm  {Mpc}^{-1}\).}}}{4}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
