\newlabel{fig:power_spectrum_comparison}{{1}{4}{\revisionreplace {Input linear matter power spectra at \(z_i=200\)}{Input primordial matter power spectra} used to generate the PL, BT\_soft, and BT\_deep initial conditions. \revisionreplace {The PL spectrum provides the standard power-law baseline. The BT spectra use the same target \(\sigma _8\) normalization and keep the PL/Eisenstein shape below their pivot scales, up to the small global normalization applied by MonofonIC; above the pivot, they depart from PL.}{The PL spectrum provides the standard power-law baseline, while the BT spectra keep the large-scale normalization fixed and depart from PL only above their respective pivot scales.} BT\_soft departs from PL over a broader range of mildly nonlinear modes, whereas BT\_deep confines the modification to smaller scales. The spectra are evaluated at the initial redshift, \(z_i=200\). \revisionreplace {For the main \(25\,h^{-1}{\rm Mpc}\), \(1024^3\) runs, \(k_{\rm Ny}\simeq 129\,h\,{\rm Mpc}^{-1}\), so the \(k_p=1\) and \(10\,h\,{\rm Mpc}^{-1}\) pivots are well below the particle-grid Nyquist scale.}{For the main $25\,h^{-1}\mathrm {Mpc}$, $1024^3$ runs, the particle-grid Nyquist scale is \(k_{\rm Ny}\simeq 129\,h\,\mathrm {Mpc}^{-1}\).}}{section*.47}{}}
