\newlabel{fig:m200c_bocquet16_hmf}{{4}{9}{Spherical-overdensity halo mass function comparison using \(M_{200c}\) masses and the \revisionreplace {Bocquet et al. (2016) fitting function}{B16 fitting function}. Symbols show the measured SOAP \(M_{200c}\) mass functions for PL, BT\_soft, and BT\_deep, while lines show the corresponding \revisionreplace {Bocquet et al. (2016) predictions}{B16 predictions} evaluated with each model's \revisionreplace {linear matter power spectrum generated from the PL or BT primordial power-spectrum template}{input power spectrum}. The SOAP catalog cut is \(N_{\rm DM}\ge 100\), and the plotted bins require \(M_{200c}\ge 2.0\times 10^8\,M_\odot \), close to the 100-particle scale \(1.9\times 10^8\,M_\odot \). The lower panels show \revisionreplace {BT\_soft/PL and BT\_deep/PL mass-function ratios}{the direct BT\_soft/PL and BT\_deep/PL mass-function ratios} in the same \(M_{200c}\) bins}{section*.96}{}}
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {replaced}{Replaced~(rev): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{The assembly shift is largest in BT\_soft. Around \(M_{z=0}\simeq 1.25\times 10^9\,M_\odot \), the mean \(z_{1/2}\) is \(2.28\) for PL, \(4.24\) for BT\_soft, and \(2.61\) for BT\_deep. Thus BT\_soft forms earlier than PL by \(\Delta z_{1/2}=1.96\), while BT\_deep forms earlier by \(0.33\), in this mass bin. Around \(M_{z=0}\simeq 7.9\times 10^9\,M_\odot \), the corresponding means are \(1.95\), \(3.12\), and \(2.05\), giving BT--PL shifts of \(1.17\) and \(0.10\). At \(M_{z=0}\simeq 2.0\times 10^{11}\,M_\odot \), the difference is smaller: \(1.43\) for PL, \(1.78\) for BT\_soft, and \(1.39\) for BT\_deep. Thus, the same model that gives the largest high-redshift HMF enhancement also shifts halo assembly earlier, especially below \(\sim 10^{10}\,M_\odot \).}}{9}{section*.99}\protected@file@percent }
