\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces \replaced [id=rev]{Concentration-derived NFW density profiles}{NFW-equivalent compactness curves} reconstructed from \textsc  {SOAP} \texttt  {SO/200\_mean} masses, radii, and concentrations for the PL, BT\_soft, and BT\_deep models. The solid lines labeled ``mean-\(c\) NFW'' use the mean concentration and radius of the halos in each mass bin to draw one NFW curve, whereas the open markers labeled ``median-\(c\) NFW'' show median curves obtained by evaluating the same NFW form halo by halo from the catalog concentrations on a common \(r/R_{200}\) grid. The markers therefore do not denote individual halos. Radii are normalized by $R_{200}$, and each panel compares halos within a \(\pm 20\%\) interval around \(M_{200m}=10^8,10^9,10^{10},\) and \(10^{11}\,M_\odot \). The panel labels report the approximate particle count at the bin center, and the dashed vertical marker shows \(\epsilon _{\rm  Pl}/R_{200}\) when it lies inside the plotted radial range. For the main-run particle mass, these bin centers correspond approximately to 50, \(5\times 10^2\), \(5\times 10^3\), and \(5\times 10^4\) particles, respectively; the \(10^8\,M_\odot \) panel is therefore closest to the resolution boundary and is interpreted qualitatively. Because both the line and marker curves are reconstructed from catalog concentrations rather than direct particle-count stacks, they should be read as \replaced [id=rev]{concentration-derived NFW profile reconstructions}{concentration-based compactness diagnostics}. \added [id=rev]{The figure is therefore not a test of NFW fit residuals for BT halo particle profiles.} \replaced [id=rev]{In this concentration-derived NFW reconstruction}{In this NFW-equivalent reconstruction}, the blue-tilted models exhibit higher central densities than PL, with the strongest effect appearing in the low- and intermediate-mass regimes.}}{12}{figure.6}\protected@file@percent }
