\newlabel{fig:halo_density_analysis}{{6}{12}{\revisionreplace {Concentration-derived NFW density profiles}{NFW-equivalent compactness curves} reconstructed from \textsc {SOAP} \texttt {SO/200\_mean} masses, radii, and concentrations for the PL, BT\_soft, and BT\_deep models. The solid lines labeled ``mean-\(c\) NFW'' use the mean concentration and radius of the halos in each mass bin to draw one NFW curve, whereas the open markers labeled ``median-\(c\) NFW'' show median curves obtained by evaluating the same NFW form halo by halo from the catalog concentrations on a common \(r/R_{200}\) grid. The markers therefore do not denote individual halos. Radii are normalized by $R_{200}$, and each panel compares halos within a \(\pm 20\%\) interval around \(M_{200m}=10^8,10^9,10^{10},\) and \(10^{11}\,M_\odot \). The panel labels report the approximate particle count at the bin center, and the dashed vertical marker shows \(\epsilon _{\rm Pl}/R_{200}\) when it lies inside the plotted radial range. For the main-run particle mass, these bin centers correspond approximately to 50, \(5\times 10^2\), \(5\times 10^3\), and \(5\times 10^4\) particles, respectively; the \(10^8\,M_\odot \) panel is therefore closest to the resolution boundary and is interpreted qualitatively. Because both the line and marker curves are reconstructed from catalog concentrations rather than direct particle-count stacks, they should be read as \revisionreplace {concentration-derived NFW profile reconstructions}{concentration-based compactness diagnostics}. \revisionblue {The figure is therefore not a test of NFW fit residuals for BT halo particle profiles.} \revisionreplace {In this concentration-derived NFW reconstruction}{In this NFW-equivalent reconstruction}, the blue-tilted models exhibit higher central densities than PL, with the strongest effect appearing in the low- and intermediate-mass regimes}{section*.118}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Concentration--mass relations for the PL, BT\_soft ($k_p=1\,h\,\mathrm  {Mpc}^{-1}$, $m_s=1.5$), and BT\_deep ($k_p=10\,h\,\mathrm  {Mpc}^{-1}$, $m_s=1.5$) models at four representative redshifts. Symbols show the simulation measurements and curves show the corresponding semi-analytic predictions. Simulation points require \(N_{\rm  DM}\ge 100\), corresponding to \(M_{200c}\gtrsim 1.9\times 10^8\,M_\odot \). The dashed and dotted vertical markers show the 100-particle selection scale and the \(\simeq 500\)-particle scale near \(10^9\,M_\odot \), respectively; quantitative low-mass concentration claims are quoted above the latter scale. At $z=0$, the median concentration in the $10^9$--$10^{9.5}\,M_\odot $ bin is higher than PL by factors of $3.20$ and $1.35$ for the $k_p=1$ and $k_p=10$ runs, respectively.}}{13}{figure.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:concentration_comparison}{{7}{13}{Concentration--mass relations for the PL, BT\_soft ($k_p=1\,h\,\mathrm {Mpc}^{-1}$, $m_s=1.5$), and BT\_deep ($k_p=10\,h\,\mathrm {Mpc}^{-1}$, $m_s=1.5$) models at four representative redshifts. Symbols show the simulation measurements and curves show the corresponding semi-analytic predictions. Simulation points require \(N_{\rm DM}\ge 100\), corresponding to \(M_{200c}\gtrsim 1.9\times 10^8\,M_\odot \). The dashed and dotted vertical markers show the 100-particle selection scale and the \(\simeq 500\)-particle scale near \(10^9\,M_\odot \), respectively; quantitative low-mass concentration claims are quoted above the latter scale. At $z=0$, the median concentration in the $10^9$--$10^{9.5}\,M_\odot $ bin is higher than PL by factors of $3.20$ and $1.35$ for the $k_p=1$ and $k_p=10$ runs, respectively}{figure.7}{}}
